      "why": "iterative is the clear area winner (best 253 vs 350 for unrolled_k, 13/15 front points). It is the only family that can plausibly hit throughput >= 1 MSPS at low area, since its 1-result-per-(N+3)-cycles schedule only works when Fmax is high enough. I narrow data_width to 12..16 and n_iter to 10..16 because the error bound 2^-10 forces a modest W and N, and larger values only inflate area without buying accuracy_bits beyond the ~10-11 the spec rewards. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..2 keep the angle/atan path and datapath tight (area is the objective) while still allowing the accuracy knobs to be explored. Both rounding modes are kept since rounding can shift max_abs_err across the 2^-10 boundary and affect accuracy_bits at near-equal area."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The fleet facts are decisive: iterative dominates on area (253 vs 350 for unrolled_k, 1332 for pipelined) and supplies 13 of 15 front points, so I concentrate the entire remaining budget on iterative and drop unrolled_k. The spec's binding constraints are throughput >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10; iterative meets these only in a narrow window where Fmax is high and N/W are small, so I focus the search there rather than re-sweeping the wide ranges already covered. I keep the ranges tight (W 12..16, N 10..16, angle_guard -2..2, frac_guard 0..2) to let Optuna resolve the area-vs-accuracy_bits trade-off near the front instead of wasting evals on obviously-too-large or obviously-inaccurate corners. The single objective pair (min luts_plus_ffs, max accuracy_bits) is well covered by this focused box, and the reserved front-mapping pass will then trace the curve over these ranges. I do not claim any specific LUT/FF/Fmax numbers; I am only choosing where to spend evaluations based on the reported bests and front composition."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current front is dominated by the `iterative` family, which suffers from high latency (N+3 cycles) and lower throughput compared to pipelined architectures. While `iterative` achieves the minimum area (253 LUTs+FFs), it is not the only feasible option. The spec requires >= 1 MSPS, which `iterative` meets easily (13.2 MSPS), but `pipelined` or `unrolled_k` families might offer better trade-offs in accuracy vs. area or allow for higher data widths within the same area budget due to different resource sharing characteristics. Specifically, `unrolled_k` with k>1 can reduce latency and potentially improve throughput while maintaining similar area to iterative if N is kept low, or `pipelined` might allow for higher n_iter (better accuracy) within the same area budget if the per-stage cost is lower. However, looking at the registry, `pipelined` has 1 result/cycle and N+2 latency. Given the area constraint is the primary objective, and `iterative` is already very efficient, exploring `unrolled_k` is a good next step to see if we can get better accuracy for the same area or lower area for the same accuracy by unrolling. Also, the current front only covers data_width 14-16. Exploring other families might reveal designs with data_width > 16 that are still feasible and offer higher accuracy bits, expanding the hypervolume. The `unrolled_k` family is a natural extension of `iterative` with a parameter `k` that can optimize the trade-off between latency/throughput and area. Let's add `unrolled_k` to the exploration.",
